<span class="ltx_personname">Wuchen Li
</span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_funding ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Mathematics, University of South Carolina, Columbia, SC, USA. <span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">wuchen@mailbox.sc.edu</span>. W. Li is partially supported by AFOSR YIP award No. FA9550-23-1-0087, NSF DMS-2245097, and NSF RTG: 2038080.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose a sparse transformer architecture that incorporates prior information about the underlying data distribution directly into the transformer structure of the neural network. The design of the model is motivated by a special optimal transport problem, namely the regularized Wasserstein proximal operator, which admits a closed-form solution and turns out to be a special representation of transformer architectures. Compared with classical flow-based models, the proposed approach improves the convexity properties of the optimization problem and promotes sparsity in the generated samples. Through both theoretical analysis and numerical experiments, including applications in generative modeling and Bayesian inverse problems, we demonstrate that the sparse transformer achieves higher accuracy and faster convergence to the target distribution than classical neural ODE–based methods.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern generative models, such as neural ordinary differential equations (neural ODEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, have demonstrated remarkable ability to learn and generate samples from complex, high-dimensional probability distributions.
These architectures have achieved broad success in scientific computing, image processing, and data science, offering scalable frameworks for data-driven modeling.
However, training and sampling in such spaces remain expensive and highly sensitive to architectural and optimization choices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advances, the curse of dimensionality continues to present a fundamental challenge in many real-world applications.
Fortunately, numerous problems in scientific computing exhibit intrinsic structures, such as sparsity, low-rank representations, or approximate invariances, that can be interpreted as prior information about the underlying data or operators.
Leveraging such priors within generative models offers a promising avenue to improve both computational efficiency and generalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A classical way to incorporate prior information, such as sparsity or piecewise regularity, is through Bayesian modeling, where the posterior combines a prior distribution encoding structural knowledge with a likelihood function derived from observations. Examples include the <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> prior in compressive sensing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and the total-variation prior in image reconstruction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
Yet embedding such priors into modern neural networks is challenging: most architectures are general-purpose approximators, and sampling from complex priors (e.g., TV) is computationally prohibitive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we embed prior information into the neural architecture through the regularized Wasserstein proximal operator (RWPO).
Classical transformer and diffusion models are largely data-driven, learning attention or score functions from data with limited connection to underlying transport dynamics.
In contrast, our approach originates from the backward regularized Wasserstein proximal (BRWP) sampling algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, developed to stabilize deterministic probability flows via alternating transport and semi-implicit proximal steps.
We scale up this sampling framework by embedding each BRWP update into a network layer: the transport step is parameterized by a learnable drift potential, and the proximal step becomes a transformer-type interaction derived from the RWPO kernel.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To formalize this idea, consider the problem of approximating a target distribution <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}\in\mathbb{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}\in\mathbb{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, representing a probability density with finite second moment, given training data drawn from <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a time horizon <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the variational problem</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\rho_{T}=\arg\min_{\rho_{T}}\min_{\tilde{\phi}_{t}}\Bigg\{\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho^{*}\|\rho_{T})+\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\!\!\int\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}_{t}\|_{2}^{2}\,\rho_{t}\,dx\,dt\Bigg\}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></munder><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{T}=\arg\min_{\rho_{T}}\min_{\tilde{\phi}_{t}}\Bigg\{\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho^{*}\|\rho_{T})+\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\!\!\int\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}_{t}\|_{2}^{2}\,\rho_{t}\,dx\,dt\Bigg\}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho^{*}\|\rho_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho^{*}\|\rho_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence between the data distribution <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and the model’s terminal distribution <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The inner minimization describes the most energy-efficient evolution of densities connecting <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> in the sense of Wasserstein-2 distance.
The corresponding constrained dynamical system yields the Fokker–Planck equation, whose particle-level evolution is given by</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="dX_{t}=\nabla\tilde{\phi}_{t}(X_{t})-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}(X_{t})\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dX_{t}=\nabla\tilde{\phi}_{t}(X_{t})-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}(X_{t})\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\phi}_{t}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\phi}_{t}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the trainable drift potential, <math id="S1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}\sim\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\sim\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}(x)=\rho(t,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}(x)=\rho(t,x)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> controls the diffusion strength.
In this formulation, the particle dynamics generalize those of a neural ODE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. The drift term <math id="S1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\tilde{\phi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\tilde{\phi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the neural network–parameterized drift potential, while the additional score term <math id="S1.p5.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> introduces a diffusion-driven regularization absent in classical neural ODEs. This score-based correction couples the flow with the Fokker–Planck evolution of the underlying density, acting as a diffusion-induced force that repels particles from high-density regions, prevents collapse, and balances the attractive drift <math id="S1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\tilde{\phi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\tilde{\phi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> toward the target distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In high-dimensional settings, estimating the score function<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> directly is computationally expensive and often unstable.
The RWPO framework circumvents this difficulty by providing a first-order approximation of the density evolution in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), while retaining a closed-form representation that approximates the score function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">More concretely, assuming <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\phi}_{t}=\phi_{t}-\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\phi}_{t}=\phi_{t}-\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, we take <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to be represented by a residual neural network and
<math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
to encode prior information on data sparsity in a time-independent potential.
Denote the tokens at time <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{k}=\{x_{j}^{k}\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{k}=\{x_{j}^{k}\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When the step size <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is small, by a first-order approximation of the RWPO kernel, the tokens (samples) evolve according to</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k+1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+h\nabla\phi_{k}(x_{j}^{k})\,,\\
x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left[\,S_{\lambda h}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}\!\left(U(x_{j}^{k+1/2},X^{k+1/2})\right)_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k+1/2}\right]\,,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mpadded style="width:3.720em;" width="3.720em"><mi>softmax</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k+1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+h\nabla\phi_{k}(x_{j}^{k})\,,\\
x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left[\,S_{\lambda h}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}\!\left(U(x_{j}^{k+1/2},X^{k+1/2})\right)_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k+1/2}\right]\,,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}=\phi_{t_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}=\phi_{t_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\lambda h}(x)=\operatorname{ReLU}(|x|-\lambda h)\quad\text{with}\quad\operatorname{ReLU}(z)=\max\{0,z\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>with</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\lambda h}(x)=\operatorname{ReLU}(|x|-\lambda h)\quad\text{with}\quad\operatorname{ReLU}(z)=\max\{0,z\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.3" class="ltx_p">is the proximal operator of the <math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm, and the interaction kernel is defined by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(x,y):=-\frac{\beta}{2}\left(\frac{\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}-\|S_{\lambda h}(x)-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}-\lambda\|S_{\lambda h}(y)\|_{1}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(x,y):=-\frac{\beta}{2}\left(\frac{\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}-\|S_{\lambda h}(x)-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}-\lambda\|S_{\lambda h}(y)\|_{1}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.4" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{softmax}(\omega)=\left(\frac{\exp(\omega_{j})}{\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\exp(\omega_{\ell})}\right)_{1\leq j\leq N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{softmax}(\omega)=\left(\frac{\exp(\omega_{j})}{\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\exp(\omega_{\ell})}\right)_{1\leq j\leq N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The second update in (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) can be viewed as a transformer-type interaction layer, which we refer to as a sparse transformer layer.
For comparison, recall that a standard transformer block (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>) with value, query, and key matrices <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> can be written in residual form as</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k}+h\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}\!\left((Qx_{j}^{k}\cdot KX^{k})\right)_{\ell}\,Vx_{\ell}^{k}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:3.720em;" width="3.720em"><mi>softmax</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k}+h\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}\!\left((Qx_{j}^{k}\cdot KX^{k})\right)_{\ell}\,Vx_{\ell}^{k}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">which can be interpreted as an interacting particle system with kernel <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Qx_{j}^{k}\!\cdot\!Kx_{\ell}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Qx_{j}^{k}\!\cdot\!Kx_{\ell}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In contrast, the proposed sparse transformer layer replaces the linear dot-product kernel with a nonlinear RWPO-based kernel where <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Qx\cdot Ky" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Qx\cdot Ky</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This substitution preserves the attention-style weighted averaging but introduces a potential-driven interaction derived from optimal-transport dynamics.
The term <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\lambda h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\lambda h}</annotation></semantics></math> enforces sparsity, while the diffusion term enhances stability.
Consequently, the layer acts as a physics-motivated analogue of transformer attention, embedding score-based diffusion into the network.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Classical approaches to generative modeling often rely on approximating score functions.
Score matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and its denoising variant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> laid the foundation for score-based generative models and diffusion methods, which learn time-dependent score functions and generate samples via reverse-time dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.
Complementary to these stochastic formulations, continuous-time viewpoints interpret learning and generation through deterministic transport flows.
Neural ODEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> view residual networks as discretizations of continuous transformations between distributions, while Langevin-type dynamics and their discretizations provide theoretically grounded sampling schemes with convergence guarantees under log-concavity assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
These frameworks emphasize two central ingredients in modern generative modeling: score estimation and space-time discretizations of transport dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Our work departs from score-estimation methods and instead builds on regularized Wasserstein gradient flows.
The Jordan–Kinderlehrer–Otto (JKO) scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> characterizes diffusion as an entropy gradient flow in Wasserstein space, linking sampling, dissipation, and variational regularization.
This viewpoint motivates Wasserstein proximal operators as stable, structure-preserving updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> and has recently inspired OT-based attention mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
From a Bayesian perspective, these proximal operators naturally encode priors via penalties such as the Lasso <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> or total variation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
Building on these ideas, our framework embeds the RWPO directly into the network, allowing priors to shape token evolution through each layer.
It unifies three principles:
(i) optimal-transport geometry via Wasserstein proximal steps approximating Fokker–Planck evolution;
(ii) Bayesian priors through explicit proximal penalties promoting sparsity; and
(iii) transformer-type interaction arising from the softmax normalization of the RWPO kernel.
The resulting sparse-transformer layer acts as a single proximal step in Wasserstein space with a closed-form attention update that preserves prior-induced structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">An overview of the remaining sections is as follows.
Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> introduces the RWPO layer and sparse transformer architecture.
Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> provides the theoretical analysis of convergence and stability for the forward dynamics and optimization problem.
Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Numerical experiments ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> demonstrates performance in generative modeling and Bayesian inverse problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Proposed network structure and PDE formulation</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Probability flow and particle updates</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For generative modeling, the central task is to generate new samples from a target distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, typically parameterized through a neural network.
We begin by recalling the KL divergence between two distributions,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho\|\rho^{*})=\int\rho\log\tfrac{\rho}{\rho^{*}}\,dx\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho\|\rho^{*})=\int\rho\log\tfrac{\rho}{\rho^{*}}\,dx\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The Wasserstein gradient flow of the KL divergence yields the Fokker–Planck equation</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\!\cdot(\rho\nabla\tilde{\phi})=\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mpadded style="width:0.663em;" width="0.663em"><mo>∇</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\!\cdot(\rho\nabla\tilde{\phi})=\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\phi}=-\log\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\phi}=-\log\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At the particle level, this corresponds to the probability flow</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="dX_{t}=\nabla\tilde{\phi}(X_{t})-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}(X_{t})\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dX_{t}=\nabla\tilde{\phi}(X_{t})-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}(X_{t})\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}\!\sim\!\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\!\sim\!\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under standard smoothness and log-concavity assumptions on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the dynamics (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) converge exponentially fast to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Equation (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) admits two complementary interpretations.
In sampling, it represents forward stochastic dynamics that asymptotically draw samples from <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> when the drift potential is known.
For generative modeling, the same drift potential can be parameterized by a neural network, transforming a simple base distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> through a sequence of deterministic updates.
This viewpoint connects probability flows with neural network architectures: the drift potential <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a trainable function governing density evolution, and each discretization step corresponds to a layer of the neural network.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, two difficulties arise when discretizing (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>):
(i) computing or approximating <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is expensive and often unstable, and
(ii) the dynamics are highly sensitive to the choice of step size.
To address these challenges, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> introduced the deterministic BRWP-splitting scheme for sampling from distributions of the form</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}(x)=\tfrac{1}{Z}\exp(\phi(x)-\psi(x))=\tfrac{1}{Z}\exp(-\tilde{\phi}(x))\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>Z</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>Z</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}(x)=\tfrac{1}{Z}\exp(\phi(x)-\psi(x))=\tfrac{1}{Z}\exp(-\tilde{\phi}(x))\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> encodes prior regularization and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\int\exp(-\tilde{\phi}(x))dx&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\int\exp(-\tilde{\phi}(x))dx&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> is a normalizing constant.
The corresponding regularized particle dynamic is</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="dX_{t}=\nabla\phi_{t}(X_{t})-\nabla\psi(X_{t})-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}(X_{t})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dX_{t}=\nabla\phi_{t}(X_{t})-\nabla\psi(X_{t})-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}(X_{t})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">A forward-Euler discretization with operator splitting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> yields the following update</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k+1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+h\nabla\phi_{k}(x_{j}^{k})\,,\\[3.0pt]
x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}-h\nabla\psi(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-h\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{k+1/2}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})\,,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>β</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k+1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+h\nabla\phi_{k}(x_{j}^{k})\,,\\[3.0pt]
x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}-h\nabla\psi(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-h\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{k+1/2}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})\,,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{k}=\{x_{j}^{k}\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{k}=\{x_{j}^{k}\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ensemble of particles at time step <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
The first step applies the drift potential induced by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second introduces the prior and diffusion corrections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Direct evaluation of the score function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> remains challenging in high dimensions.
To stabilize the dynamics and avoid explicit score estimation, we replace the second step in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) with a semi-implicit update based on the RWPO.
The resulting scheme, whose derivation is detailed below, evolves as</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k+1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+h\nabla\phi(x_{j}^{k})\,,\\[3.0pt]
x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left[\,\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}\!\big(U(x_{j}^{k+1/2},X^{k+1/2})\big)_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k+1/2}\right]\!,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>prox</mtext><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mpadded style="width:3.720em;" width="3.720em"><mi>softmax</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo>]</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k+1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+h\nabla\phi(x_{j}^{k})\,,\\[3.0pt]
x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left[\,\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}\!\big(U(x_{j}^{k+1/2},X^{k+1/2})\big)_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k+1/2}\right]\!,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>prox</mtext><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is the proximal operator of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}(x):=\argmin_{y}\Big\{\psi(y)+\tfrac{\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}\Big\},\quad U(x,y):=-\tfrac{\beta}{2}\!\left(\tfrac{\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}-\|\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}(x)-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}-\psi(\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}(y))\right)\!." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>prox</mtext><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>prox</mtext><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>prox</mtext><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo>)</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}(x):=\argmin_{y}\Big\{\psi(y)+\tfrac{\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}\Big\},\quad U(x,y):=-\tfrac{\beta}{2}\!\left(\tfrac{\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}-\|\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}(x)-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}-\psi(\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}(y))\right)\!.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">This formulation combines closed-form proximal updates with a semi-implicit treatment of the score term, allowing large, stable time steps without explicitly computing <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The interaction kernel <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> arises from a Laplace approximation of the RWPO operator, as described in the next subsection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the original BRWP scheme, the flow alternates between a transport step guided by the drift potential and a proximal step that enforces prior regularization, yielding a stable deterministic sampling algorithm.
Here, we reinterpret each BRWP update as a neural network layer: the transport step becomes a learnable map <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\phi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\phi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by a ResNet, while the proximal step is realized as a transformer-type interaction derived from the RWPO kernel.
This construction converts the sampling algorithm into a deep, scalable architecture for generative modeling.
To emphasize the connection with attention mechanisms, the particles <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are referred to as <em id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tokens</em>. The first update in (<a href="#S2.E9" title="In 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) acts as a learnable transport map, and the second enforces prior-induced regularization with diffusion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The overall network structure is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, showing how the learned drift potential <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is coupled with an RWPO-based sparse-transformer layer that enforces sparsity through proximal updates.
A detailed derivation follows.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/transformer.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/411;" width="476" height="411" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Network structure: the drift potential <math id="S2.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is approximated by a ResNet, followed by a sparse-transformer (RWPO) layer that enforces sparsity through proximal updates.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Regularized Wasserstein proximal operator layer</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now detail the derivation of the system (<a href="#S2.E9" title="In 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) and update rule underlying Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The main numerical difficulty in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) lies in the unstable approximation of the score function <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To mitigate this, we adopt a semi-implicit strategy by evaluating the score at the next time step <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, replacing the second update in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) with</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}-h\nabla\psi(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-h\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{k+1}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}-h\nabla\psi(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-h\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{k+1}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Formally, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be characterized through the JKO scheme, which requires solving a high-dimensional variational problem.
To obtain a tractable approximation, we instead employ the <em id="S2.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">regularized Wasserstein proximal operator</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, defined as</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{h}_{\psi}(\rho_{k}):=\argmin_{\rho_{h}}\,\min_{v,\rho}\int\psi(x)\,\rho_{h}(x)\,dx+\tfrac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{h}\!\!\int\|v(x,t)\|_{2}^{2}\rho(x,t)\,dx\,dt," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>dx</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>dx</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>dt</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{h}_{\psi}(\rho_{k}):=\argmin_{\rho_{h}}\,\min_{v,\rho}\int\psi(x)\,\rho_{h}(x)\,dx+\tfrac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{h}\!\!\int\|v(x,t)\|_{2}^{2}\rho(x,t)\,dx\,dt,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">subject to the Fokker–Planck equation (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\tilde{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\tilde{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, initial condition <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(x,0)=\rho_{k}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(x,0)=\rho_{k}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and terminal condition <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{h}(x)=\rho(x,h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{h}(x)=\rho(x,h)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We assume the minimization admits a solution under standard regularity conditions on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From the analysis in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, the RWPO provides a second-order accurate approximation of the JKO update:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{h}_{\psi}(\rho_{k+1/2})=\rho_{k+1}+\mathcal{O}(h^{2})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{h}_{\psi}(\rho_{k+1/2})=\rho_{k+1}+\mathcal{O}(h^{2})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This justifies replacing <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E10" title="In 2.2 Regularized Wasserstein proximal operator layer ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) with its RWPO approximation, leading to a stable and implementable update.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is nonsmooth (e.g., <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>), we denote by <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\psi</annotation></semantics></math> its subdifferential.
The gradient step then becomes the proximal relation</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{x-\text{prox}^{h}_{\psi}(x)}{h}\in\partial\psi(x)\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>prox</mtext><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{x-\text{prox}^{h}_{\psi}(x)}{h}\in\partial\psi(x)\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which ensures consistency with the nonsmooth regularization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Combining the drift and proximal corrections yields the complete token updates</p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k+1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+h\nabla\phi_{k}(x_{j}^{k})\,,\\[3.0pt]
x_{j}^{k+1}=\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-h\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\mathcal{K}^{h}_{\psi}\rho_{k+1/2}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})\,,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext>prox</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ψ</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>β</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k+1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+h\nabla\phi_{k}(x_{j}^{k})\,,\\[3.0pt]
x_{j}^{k+1}=\text{prox}_{\psi}^{h}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-h\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\mathcal{K}^{h}_{\psi}\rho_{k+1/2}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})\,,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which defines a semi-implicit update integrating prior regularization and the regularized Wasserstein proximal operator.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Sparse transformer with <math id="S2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> prior</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now specialize the RWPO layer to the case where the target distribution exhibits sparsity, a common scenario in Bayesian inverse problems and image reconstruction.
A classical choice of sparsity-promoting prior is the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> potential <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For this choice, the proximal operator takes the soft-thresholding form</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{\lambda h}(x_{i})=\text{prox}_{\lambda\|\cdot\|_{1}}^{h}(x)_{i}=\begin{cases}x_{i}-\lambda h&amp;\text{if }x_{i}&gt;\lambda h,\\
0&amp;\text{if }|x_{i}|\leq\lambda h,\\
x_{i}+\lambda h&amp;\text{if }x_{i}&lt;-\lambda h\,,\end{cases}=\mathrm{ReLU}(|x_{i}|-\lambda h)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mtext>prox</mtext><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ReLU</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\lambda h}(x_{i})=\text{prox}_{\lambda\|\cdot\|_{1}}^{h}(x)_{i}=\begin{cases}x_{i}-\lambda h&amp;\text{if }x_{i}&gt;\lambda h,\\
0&amp;\text{if }|x_{i}|\leq\lambda h,\\
x_{i}+\lambda h&amp;\text{if }x_{i}&lt;-\lambda h\,,\end{cases}=\mathrm{ReLU}(|x_{i}|-\lambda h)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we recall a general closed-form expression for the RWPO operator, which serves as the foundation for constructing token-interaction kernels.
The result follows from applying the Hopf–Cole transform, whose proof can be found in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a convex potential, and let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}\in\mathbb{P}^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}\in\mathbb{P}^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the regularized Wasserstein proximal operator (<a href="#S2.E11" title="In 2.2 Regularized Wasserstein proximal operator layer ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) admits the integral representation</span></p>
<table id="S2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{h}_{\psi}\rho_{k}(x)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\frac{\exp\!\left[-\tfrac{\beta}{2}\!\left(\psi(x)+\tfrac{\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}\right)\right]}{\displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\!\exp\!\left[-\tfrac{\beta}{2}\!\left(\psi(z)+\tfrac{\|z-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}\right)\right]dz}\,\rho_{k}(y)\,dy\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{h}_{\psi}\rho_{k}(x)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\frac{\exp\!\left[-\tfrac{\beta}{2}\!\left(\psi(x)+\tfrac{\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}\right)\right]}{\displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\!\exp\!\left[-\tfrac{\beta}{2}\!\left(\psi(z)+\tfrac{\|z-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}\right)\right]dz}\,\rho_{k}(y)\,dy\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the kernel depends on <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, the step size <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the inverse temperature constant (diffusion strength) <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The representation (<a href="#S2.E13" title="In Lemma 1. ‣ 2.3 Sparse transformer with 𝐿_1 prior ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) involves a high-dimensional integral that is generally intractable.
In practice, we approximate <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by the empirical measure
<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}(x)\approx\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\delta_{x^{k}_{j}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}(x)\approx\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\delta_{x^{k}_{j}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>
and apply the Laplace approximation to obtain a computable first-order estimate of the score function.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the score function associated with <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{h}_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{h}_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the first-order approximation</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\!\left|\nabla\log\mathcal{K}_{\psi}^{h}\rho_{k+1/2}(x)-\left[-\frac{\beta}{2}\!\left(\frac{x-S_{\lambda h}(x)}{h}+\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{N}(x-x_{j}^{k+1/2})\exp(U(x,x_{j}^{k+1/2}))}{h\sum_{j=1}^{N}\exp(U(x,x_{j}^{k+1/2}))}\right)\right]\!\right|=0\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo>]</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\!\left|\nabla\log\mathcal{K}_{\psi}^{h}\rho_{k+1/2}(x)-\left[-\frac{\beta}{2}\!\left(\frac{x-S_{\lambda h}(x)}{h}+\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{N}(x-x_{j}^{k+1/2})\exp(U(x,x_{j}^{k+1/2}))}{h\sum_{j=1}^{N}\exp(U(x,x_{j}^{k+1/2}))}\right)\right]\!\right|=0\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\lambda h}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\lambda h}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the soft-thresholding operator and</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(x,y):=-\frac{\beta}{2}\!\left(\frac{\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}-\|S_{\lambda h}(x)-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}-\lambda\|S_{\lambda h}(y)\|_{1}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(x,y):=-\frac{\beta}{2}\!\left(\frac{\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}-\|S_{\lambda h}(x)-y\|_{2}^{2}}{2h}-\lambda\|S_{\lambda h}(y)\|_{1}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently, the second update step in (<a href="#S2.E12" title="In 2.2 Regularized Wasserstein proximal operator layer ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) reduces to</span></p>
<table id="S2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left[S_{\lambda h}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}(U(x_{j}^{k+1/2},\,X^{k+1/2}))_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k+1/2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left[S_{\lambda h}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}(U(x_{j}^{k+1/2},\,X^{k+1/2}))_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k+1/2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The result follows by isolating the dominant contribution of the kernel integral in (<a href="#S2.E13" title="In Lemma 1. ‣ 2.3 Sparse transformer with 𝐿_1 prior ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) as <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> for details.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with a non-degenerate minimizer, the approximation error in (<a href="#S2.E14" title="In Proposition 2. ‣ 2.3 Sparse transformer with 𝐿_1 prior ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) is <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> by the standard Laplace method, with the leading correction determined by the local Hessian of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is only Lipschitz—as in the <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> case—the limit in (<a href="#S2.E14" title="In Proposition 2. ‣ 2.3 Sparse transformer with 𝐿_1 prior ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) still holds because the score function can be expressed as <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla\log\mathcal{K}_{\psi}^{h}\rho=(\nabla\mathcal{K}_{\psi}^{h}\rho)/(\mathcal{K}_{\psi}^{h}\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log\mathcal{K}_{\psi}^{h}\rho=(\nabla\mathcal{K}_{\psi}^{h}\rho)/(\mathcal{K}_{\psi}^{h}\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>, where normalization constants cancel without requiring smoothness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The closed-form RWPO update (<a href="#S2.E15" title="In Proposition 2. ‣ 2.3 Sparse transformer with 𝐿_1 prior ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) thus defines the <em id="S2.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sparse-transformer layer</em>, in which the RWPO replaces the standard dot-product attention with an optimal-transport kernel as discussed in (<a href="#S1.E4" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), embedding structural priors directly into the network. Furthermore, because the RWPO update admits closed-form proximal and softmax evaluations, the computational cost per layer is comparable to standard transformer layer, ensuring scalability to large token sets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Learning objective and training procedure</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now make precise the training objective used to learn the unknown drift potential <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> with prior <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>. The loss function we propose combines three components:</p>
<table id="S2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\phi):=\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho^{*}\|\rho_{T})\;+\;\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}(x,t)\|_{2}^{2}\,\rho(x,t)\,dx\,dt\;+\;c\,R(T)\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\phi):=\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho^{*}\|\rho_{T})\;+\;\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}(x,t)\|_{2}^{2}\,\rho(x,t)\,dx\,dt\;+\;c\,R(T)\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the terminal density produced by the dynamics;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is a weight; and <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is an HJB residual regularizer (defined below) that penalizes deviations from the optimality conditions associated with the variational formulation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">For the continuous dynamics, the training objective can be formulated as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{\rho}\,\mathcal{J}(\phi)\quad\text{subject to}\quad dX_{t}=\nabla{\phi}_{t}(X_{t})-\nabla{\tilde{\phi}}(X_{t})-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}(X_{t})\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>ρ</mi></munder><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>subject to</mtext><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\rho}\,\mathcal{J}(\phi)\quad\text{subject to}\quad dX_{t}=\nabla{\phi}_{t}(X_{t})-\nabla{\tilde{\phi}}(X_{t})-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}(X_{t})\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}\sim\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\sim\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\tilde{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\tilde{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is approximated by a proximal operator for non-smooth prior drift potential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We remark that, relative to the variational formulation (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), the additional HJB regularizer vanishes at the optimal solution and thus primarily serves to accelerate convergence without modifying the minimizer.
In the following, we explain the role of each term in (<a href="#S2.E16" title="In 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) and present the corresponding discrete Monte Carlo estimators used during training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(I) The KL divergence and its evaluation along trajectories.</span>
The KL divergence can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho^{*}\|\rho_{T})=\int\rho^{*}(x)\log\frac{\rho^{*}(x)}{\rho_{T}(x)}\,dx=\int\rho^{*}(x)\log\rho^{*}(x)\,dx-\int\rho^{*}(x)\log\rho_{T}(x)\,dx\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho^{*}\|\rho_{T})=\int\rho^{*}(x)\log\frac{\rho^{*}(x)}{\rho_{T}(x)}\,dx=\int\rho^{*}(x)\log\rho^{*}(x)\,dx-\int\rho^{*}(x)\log\rho_{T}(x)\,dx\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Since the first term is independent of the model, minimizing the KL divergence is equivalent to maximizing the log-likelihood term
<math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\rho^{*}(x)\log\rho_{T}(x)\,dx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\rho^{*}(x)\log\rho_{T}(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the simplicity of notation, let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v(x,t)=\nabla\phi(x,t)-\nabla\psi(x)-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho(x,t)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(x,t)=\nabla\phi(x,t)-\nabla\psi(x)-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho(x,t)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">To estimate the expectation with respect to <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we draw training samples at the terminal time,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{j}^{M}\sim\rho^{*},\qquad j=1,\dots,N\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>M</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{M}\sim\rho^{*},\qquad j=1,\dots,N\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">and evaluate <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\rho_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\rho_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> at these points. This requires recovering the full trajectory <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{j}^{k}\}_{k=0}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{j}^{k}\}_{k=0}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, we integrate the backward ODE</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}x_{t}=-v(x_{t},t)\,,\qquad x_{T}=x_{j}^{M}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}x_{t}=-v(x_{t},t)\,,\qquad x_{T}=x_{j}^{M}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.4" class="ltx_p">from <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T</annotation></semantics></math> down to <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding preimage with density <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For completeness, we recall the log-density evolution along forward trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Log-density evolution along trajectories)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the transport equation</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\!\cdot(\rho v)=0\,,\qquad\frac{d}{dt}x_{t}=v(x_{t},t)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.663em;" width="0.663em"><mo>∇</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\!\cdot(\rho v)=0\,,\qquad\frac{d}{dt}x_{t}=v(x_{t},t)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then along any forward trajectory <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> solving <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dx_{t}/dt=v(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dx_{t}/dt=v(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, the material derivative of the log-density satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{t}\log\rho(x_{t},t)\;=\;-\nabla\!\cdot v(x_{t},t)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.058em">=</mo><mo>−</mo><mpadded style="width:0.663em;" width="0.663em"><mo>∇</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}\log\rho(x_{t},t)\;=\;-\nabla\!\cdot v(x_{t},t)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, if <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v(x,t)=\nabla\phi(x,t)-\nabla\psi(x)-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho(x,t)\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(x,t)=\nabla\phi(x,t)-\nabla\psi(x)-\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho(x,t)\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{t}\log\rho(x_{t},t)=\nabla\!\cdot\big(-\nabla\phi(x_{t},t)+\nabla\psi(x_{t})+\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho(x_{t},t)\big)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:0.663em;" width="0.663em"><mo>∇</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}\log\rho(x_{t},t)=\nabla\!\cdot\big(-\nabla\phi(x_{t},t)+\nabla\psi(x_{t})+\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho(x_{t},t)\big)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">By Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3 (Log-density evolution along trajectories). ‣ 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, for each trajectory we have the identity</p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\rho_{T}(x_{j}(T))\;=\;\log\rho_{0}(x_{j}(0))\;-\;\int_{0}^{T}\nabla\!\cdot v(x_{j}(t),t)\,dt\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.335em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mpadded style="width:0.663em;" width="0.663em"><mo>∇</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\rho_{T}(x_{j}(T))\;=\;\log\rho_{0}(x_{j}(0))\;-\;\int_{0}^{T}\nabla\!\cdot v(x_{j}(t),t)\,dt\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, the log-likelihood term can be approximated by a Riemann sum with step size <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=T/M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=T/M</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int\rho^{*}(x)\log\rho_{T}(x)\,dx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int\rho^{*}(x)\log\rho_{T}(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;\approx\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\log\rho_{T}(x_{j}^{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;\approx\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\log\rho_{T}(x_{j}^{M})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;=\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\Big[\log\rho_{0}(x_{j}^{0})-h\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}\big(\nabla\!\cdot v\big)(x_{j}^{k},\,t_{k})\Big]\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.663em;" width="0.663em"><mo>∇</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.170em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;=\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\Big[\log\rho_{0}(x_{j}^{0})-h\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}\big(\nabla\!\cdot v\big)(x_{j}^{k},\,t_{k})\Big]\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{M}\sim\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{M}\sim\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{j}^{k}\}_{k=0}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{j}^{k}\}_{k=0}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained by backward integration of the dynamics (e.g., via the splitting scheme (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.1 Probability flow and particle updates ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)), and <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a known simple distribution, such as a Gaussian distribution. In practice, the divergence <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\!\cdot v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.663em;" width="0.663em"><mo>∇</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\!\cdot v</annotation></semantics></math> is computed either by automatic differentiation or by closed-form formulas, depending on the parametrization of <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(II) The transport regularizer.</span>
The second term in (<a href="#S2.E16" title="In 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}(x,t)\|_{2}^{2}\,\rho(x,t)\,dx\,dt\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}(x,t)\|_{2}^{2}\,\rho(x,t)\,dx\,dt\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">penalizes the kinetic energy of the transportation vector field, which promotes smooth, energy-efficient token transports.
For <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}(x)=\phi(x,t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}(x)=\phi(x,t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, the time-space integral is approximated using a standard Monte Carlo Riemann-sum scheme:</p>
<table id="S2.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}\rho\,dx\,dt\;\approx\;\frac{h}{2N}\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\big\lVert\nabla\tilde{\phi}_{k}(x_{j}^{k})\big\rVert_{2}^{2}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}\rho\,dx\,dt\;\approx\;\frac{h}{2N}\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\big\lVert\nabla\tilde{\phi}_{k}(x_{j}^{k})\big\rVert_{2}^{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(III) The HJB regularizer.</span>
The third term, <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, enforces consistency with the Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman (HJB) equation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, which arises from the optimality condition for <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) (see (<a href="#S3.E33" title="In 3.2 Stability of the optimization problem ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Stability of the optimization problem ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> for the derivation). The HJB residual at state <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{HJB}}(x,t):=\partial_{t}\phi(x,t)+\tfrac{1}{2}\big\lVert\nabla\phi(x,t)-\nabla\psi(x)\big\rVert_{2}^{2}+\beta^{-1}\big(\Delta\phi(x,t)-\Delta\psi(x)\big)\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>HJB</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{HJB}}(x,t):=\partial_{t}\phi(x,t)+\tfrac{1}{2}\big\lVert\nabla\phi(x,t)-\nabla\psi(x)\big\rVert_{2}^{2}+\beta^{-1}\big(\Delta\phi(x,t)-\Delta\psi(x)\big)\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">and the optimality condition of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) implies <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{HJB}}\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>HJB</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{HJB}}\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The HJB regularizer is then</p>
<table id="S2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(T)\;:=\;\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\big|\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{HJB}}(x,t)\big|^{2}\rho(x,t)\,dx\,dt\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>HJB</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(T)\;:=\;\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\big|\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{HJB}}(x,t)\big|^{2}\rho(x,t)\,dx\,dt\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p8.3" class="ltx_p">which we discretize analogously to the previous terms</p>
<table id="S2.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(T)\;\approx\;\frac{h}{N}\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\big|\partial_{t}\phi_{k}(x^{k}_{j})+\tfrac{1}{2}\big\lVert\nabla\phi_{k}(x^{k}_{j})-\nabla\psi(x^{k}_{j})\big\rVert_{2}^{2}+\beta^{-1}\big(\Delta\phi_{k}(x^{k}_{j})-\Delta\psi(x^{k}_{j})\big)\big|^{2}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(T)\;\approx\;\frac{h}{N}\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\big|\partial_{t}\phi_{k}(x^{k}_{j})+\tfrac{1}{2}\big\lVert\nabla\phi_{k}(x^{k}_{j})-\nabla\psi(x^{k}_{j})\big\rVert_{2}^{2}+\beta^{-1}\big(\Delta\phi_{k}(x^{k}_{j})-\Delta\psi(x^{k}_{j})\big)\big|^{2}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p8.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is evaluated by the smooth approximation of <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., Huber function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remarks.</span></p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The spatial differential operators required for the transport and HJB regularizers are already computed when evaluating the KL divergence, so including these regularizers incurs no additional differentiation cost.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objective (<a href="#S2.E16" title="In 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) combines three components: the KL divergence enforces likelihood matching, the transport regularizer encourages energy-efficient mapping consistent with optimal transport, and the HJB regularizer ensures dynamic consistency with the underlying variational formulation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">With discrete estimators for the loss function given in (<a href="#S2.Ex19" title="In 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>), (<a href="#S2.E19" title="In 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), and (<a href="#S2.E21" title="In 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>), algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes both the generation and training stages corresponding to the forward and backward RWPO updates.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Sparse Transformer with RWPO Operator: Generation and Training</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Time stamps <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{k}\}_{k=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{k}\}_{k=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, training data <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{T}_{j}\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{T}_{j}\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, maximal training iterations <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:345.0pt;height:0.6pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generation phase (with trained drift potential <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:345.0pt;height:0.6pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            Sample initial tokens <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{0}\sim\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{0}\sim\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,\dots,M-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,\dots,M-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              Propagate tokens using RWPO splitting scheme:

<table id="S2.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k+1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+h\,\nabla\phi_{k}(x_{j}^{k})\,,\\
x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\left[\,S_{\lambda h}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}(U(x_{j}^{k+1/2},X^{k+1/2}))_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k+1/2}\right]\,.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k+1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+h\,\nabla\phi_{k}(x_{j}^{k})\,,\\
x_{j}^{k+1}=x_{j}^{k+1/2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\left[\,S_{\lambda h}(x_{j}^{k+1/2})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}(U(x_{j}^{k+1/2},X^{k+1/2}))_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k+1/2}\right]\,.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
            <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:345.0pt;height:0.6pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training phase</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
            <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:345.0pt;height:0.6pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
            Initialize <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq L_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq L_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
              Construct loss <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> using the summation of (<a href="#S2.Ex19" title="In 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>), (<a href="#S2.E19" title="In 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), (<a href="#S2.E21" title="In 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=M,\dots,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=M,\dots,1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
               Update tokens via RWPO splitting:

<table id="S2.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k-1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+\tfrac{1}{2}\left[\,S_{\lambda h}(x_{j}^{k})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}(U(x_{j}^{k},X^{k}))_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k}\right],\\
x_{j}^{k-1}=x_{j}^{k-1/2}-h\,\nabla\phi_{k-1/2}(x_{j}^{k-1/2})\,.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}x_{j}^{k-1/2}=x_{j}^{k}+\tfrac{1}{2}\left[\,S_{\lambda h}(x_{j}^{k})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}(U(x_{j}^{k},X^{k}))_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k}\right],\\
x_{j}^{k-1}=x_{j}^{k-1/2}-h\,\nabla\phi_{k-1/2}(x_{j}^{k-1/2})\,.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
              
               Using Prop. <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3 (Log-density evolution along trajectories). ‣ 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, update log-densities along trajectories:

<table id="S2.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\log\rho_{k-1/2}(x_{j}^{k-1/2})=\log\rho_{k}(x_{j}^{k})-\frac{1}{2}\nabla\cdot\left[\,S_{\lambda h}(x_{j}^{k})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}(U(x_{j}^{k},X^{k}))_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k}\right]\,,\\
\log\rho_{k-1}(x_{j}^{k-1})=\log\rho_{k-1/2}(x_{j}^{k-1/2})+h\,\nabla\cdot\nabla\phi_{k-1/2}(x_{j}^{k-1/2})\,.\\
\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mo>∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mi>softmax</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\log\rho_{k-1/2}(x_{j}^{k-1/2})=\log\rho_{k}(x_{j}^{k})-\frac{1}{2}\nabla\cdot\left[\,S_{\lambda h}(x_{j}^{k})-\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\operatorname{softmax}(U(x_{j}^{k},X^{k}))_{\ell}\,x_{\ell}^{k}\right]\,,\\
\log\rho_{k-1}(x_{j}^{k-1})=\log\rho_{k-1/2}(x_{j}^{k-1/2})+h\,\nabla\cdot\nabla\phi_{k-1/2}(x_{j}^{k-1/2})\,.\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l18.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">19:</span></span>
              
              
              Compute gradient <math id="alg1.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> and update parameters with a first-order optimizer (such as Adam):

<table id="S2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\leftarrow\text{OptimizerStep}(\theta,\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>OptimizerStep</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\leftarrow\text{OptimizerStep}(\theta,\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">20:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow i+1</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l21.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">21:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l21.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide a rigorous analysis of why the sparse transformer proposed in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> achieves faster and more robust convergence toward sparse target distributions. Our analysis proceeds along two complementary directions. First, in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we study how the additional sparse prior directly accelerates long-time convergence of the dynamics, as measured by the KL divergence. Second, in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Stability of the optimization problem ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we analyze the variational optimization problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and reveal the stabilizing role of viscosity. The proof of results in this section can be found in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Convergence with the sparse prior</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We examine the effect of the sparse prior <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
on the evolution of</p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\rho\;=\;\nabla\!\cdot\!\big(\rho\,(-\nabla\phi+\nabla\psi^{\lambda})\big)\;+\;\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.663em;" width="0.663em"><mo>∇</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\rho\;=\;\nabla\!\cdot\!\big(\rho\,(-\nabla\phi+\nabla\psi^{\lambda})\big)\;+\;\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The analysis proceeds in two steps. First, we study the decay of the KL divergence to show that the sparse prior accelerates convergence to the target. Second, we investigate the evolution of the second moment to quantify distributional contraction; larger values of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> enhance both effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with KL decay under an exact drift potential. Suppose <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen so that <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}(x)\propto e^{\beta\phi(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}(x)\propto e^{\beta\phi(x)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the forward KL dissipation satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}\,\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})=-\beta^{-1}\int\big\|\nabla\log\tfrac{\rho_{t}}{\rho^{*}}\big\|^{2}\rho_{t}\,dx\;-\;\lambda\int\nabla\log\tfrac{\rho_{t}}{\rho^{*}}\cdot\operatorname{sign}(x)\,\rho_{t}\,dx\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>∫</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>sign</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}\,\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})=-\beta^{-1}\int\big\|\nabla\log\tfrac{\rho_{t}}{\rho^{*}}\big\|^{2}\rho_{t}\,dx\;-\;\lambda\int\nabla\log\tfrac{\rho_{t}}{\rho^{*}}\cdot\operatorname{sign}(x)\,\rho_{t}\,dx\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sign}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sign}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes any measurable selection of the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> subgradient and is justified via smooth approximations. Thus the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> prior contributes the nonnegative term</p>
<table id="S3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})\;:=\;\int\nabla\log\tfrac{\rho_{t}}{\rho^{*}}\cdot\operatorname{sign}(x)\,\rho_{t}\,dx\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})\;:=\;\int\nabla\log\tfrac{\rho_{t}}{\rho^{*}}\cdot\operatorname{sign}(x)\,\rho_{t}\,dx\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which accelerates convergence whenever <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, we assume the following lower bound, referred to as <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">directional consistency</em>:</p>
<table id="S3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})\;\geq\;\gamma\sqrt{\,I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})\,}\,,\quad\forall t\geq 0\,,\quad\text{ where }\qquad I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*}):=\int\|\nabla\log(\rho_{t}/\rho^{*})\|_{2}^{2}\rho_{t}\,dx\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mi>γ</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext> where </mtext><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})\;\geq\;\gamma\sqrt{\,I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})\,}\,,\quad\forall t\geq 0\,,\quad\text{ where }\qquad I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*}):=\int\|\nabla\log(\rho_{t}/\rho^{*})\|_{2}^{2}\rho_{t}\,dx\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">for some <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> is the relative Fisher information. The property (<a href="#S3.E27" title="In 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) holds for several common parametric families (e.g., Laplace and Gaussian families), as illustrated below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Laplace distributions.</em> For <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}(x)=\tfrac{b}{2}e^{-b|x|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}(x)=\tfrac{b}{2}e^{-b|x|}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}(x)=\tfrac{a}{2}e^{-a|x|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}(x)=\tfrac{a}{2}e^{-a|x|}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})=b-a\,,\qquad I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})=(a-b)^{2}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})=b-a\,,\qquad I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})=(a-b)^{2}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">hence <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})=\operatorname{sign}(b-a)\sqrt{I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})=\operatorname{sign}(b-a)\sqrt{I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})}</annotation></semantics></math> and (<a href="#S3.E27" title="In 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) holds with <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> exactly when <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gaussian distributions.</em> For <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}=\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}=\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}=\mathcal{N}(0,\tau^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}=\mathcal{N}(0,\tau^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, writing <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:=\tfrac{1}{\sigma^{2}}-\tfrac{1}{\tau^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:=\tfrac{1}{\sigma^{2}}-\tfrac{1}{\tau^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="S3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})=\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{\pi}}\,c\,\tau\,,\qquad I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})=c^{2}\tau^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mi>c</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})=\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{\pi}}\,c\,\tau\,,\qquad I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})=c^{2}\tau^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">so <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})=\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{\pi}}\,\operatorname{sign}(c)\sqrt{I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})=\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{\pi}}\,\operatorname{sign}(c)\sqrt{I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})}</annotation></semantics></math> and (<a href="#S3.E27" title="In 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) holds with <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\sqrt{2/\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\sqrt{2/\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}\geq\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}\geq\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies a log-Sobolev inequality with constant <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{LS}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>LS</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{LS}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose (<a href="#S3.E27" title="In 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) holds with some <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a:=\tfrac{2}{C_{\mathrm{LS}}}\,,\qquad y(t):=\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>LS</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:=\tfrac{2}{C_{\mathrm{LS}}}\,,\qquad y(t):=\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, for all <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and denoting <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x)_{+}=\max\{x,0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x)_{+}=\max\{x,0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(t)\;\leq\;\Bigg(\Big(\sqrt{y(0)}+\tfrac{\lambda\gamma}{\sqrt{a}}\Big)e^{-\tfrac{a}{2}t}-\tfrac{\lambda\gamma}{\sqrt{a}}\Bigg)_{+}^{2}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(t)\;\leq\;\Bigg(\Big(\sqrt{y(0)}+\tfrac{\lambda\gamma}{\sqrt{a}}\Big)e^{-\tfrac{a}{2}t}-\tfrac{\lambda\gamma}{\sqrt{a}}\Bigg)_{+}^{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To summarize, the additional term <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\lambda\gamma/\sqrt{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>γ</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\lambda\gamma/\sqrt{a}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E28" title="In Proposition 4. ‣ 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>) introduces an extra linear damping effect on <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{y(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{y(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby accelerating the exponential decay of the KL divergence.
Larger values of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to stronger sparse priors, which in turn yield faster contraction of the dynamics toward the target distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the estimate (<a href="#S3.E28" title="In Proposition 4. ‣ 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>) reduces to the classical exponential decay rate implied by the log-Sobolev inequality without prior regularization.
Below, we give more examples of when the directional consistency condition (<a href="#S3.E27" title="In 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Separable / product families.</em> If <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are products of 1D laws and directional consistency holds in each coordinate with constants <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})\geq(\min_{j}\kappa_{j})\sqrt{I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})}=\gamma\sqrt{I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>j</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})\geq(\min_{j}\kappa_{j})\sqrt{I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})}=\gamma\sqrt{I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">General principle.</em> Directional consistency occurs when the prior drift <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and the score function <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log(\rho_{t}/\rho^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log(\rho_{t}/\rho^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> point roughly in the same correcting direction. If <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is more spread out than <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, both vectors point inward, yielding <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})&gt;0\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})&gt;0\,.</annotation></semantics></math> However, misalignment can lead to negative correlation, which breaks the condition (<a href="#S3.E27" title="In 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">These examples show that directional consistency naturally holds when the target distribution is concentrated. Increasing <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> strengthens the linear drift potential in the ODE for <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{y(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{y(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which accelerates convergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond convergence in KL divergence, next, we show that the same <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> prior introduces additional mass contraction, which we quantify via the second moment.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> and denote <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> as the solution to (<a href="#S3.E25" title="In 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) with <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.8em;">
<li id="S3.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any fixed drift potential </span><math id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}\int\|x\|_{2}^{2}\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx=-2\lambda\int\|x\|_{1}\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx+2\int x\cdot\nabla\phi(x)\,\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx+2d\beta^{-1}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∫</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}\int\|x\|_{2}^{2}\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx=-2\lambda\int\|x\|_{1}\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx+2\int x\cdot\nabla\phi(x)\,\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx+2d\beta^{-1}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, the first term implies that a larger </span><math id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> accelerates the decay of the second moment.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\geq\lambda_{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\geq\lambda_{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)=-\tfrac{1}{2}x^{\top}Ax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)=-\tfrac{1}{2}x^{\top}Ax</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\succeq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⪰</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\succeq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\|x\|_{2}^{2}\rho_{t}^{\lambda_{1}}(x)\,dx\;\leq\;\int\|x\|_{2}^{2}\rho_{t}^{\lambda_{2}}(x)\,dx\,,\qquad\forall\,t\geq 0\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">≤</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\|x\|_{2}^{2}\rho_{t}^{\lambda_{1}}(x)\,dx\;\leq\;\int\|x\|_{2}^{2}\rho_{t}^{\lambda_{2}}(x)\,dx\,,\qquad\forall\,t\geq 0\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remarks.</span>
The <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> term serves as an additional damping strength in the dynamics.
The first term on the right-hand side of (<a href="#S3.E29" title="In item (i) ‣ Proposition 5. ‣ 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-2\lambda\int\|x\|_{1}\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2\lambda\int\|x\|_{1}\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">acts as a dissipative force that contracts trajectories toward the origin, thereby reducing the spread of <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> and accelerating the decay of its second moment.
Larger values of <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> amplify this contraction.
Together with the KL dissipation analysis, this demonstrates that the sparse <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> prior not only accelerates convergence in a distributional sense but also enforces a tighter concentration of mass.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Stability of the optimization problem</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now examine the variational well-posedness of the learning problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and its associated token dynamics (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
In particular, we show that the viscosity term arising from the score function prevents gradient blow-up in the backward HJB equation, ensuring the stability of both the dynamics and the variational formulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To reformulate (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) as an unconstrained problem, we introduce a Lagrange multiplier <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>. The constrained formulation then becomes</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\argmin_{\rho_{T}}\;\min_{\Phi,\tilde{\phi},\rho}\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.447em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\argmin_{\rho_{T}}\;\min_{\Phi,\tilde{\phi},\rho}\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E31.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho^{*}\|\rho_{T})+\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}\,\rho\,dx\,dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(\rho^{*}\|\rho_{T})+\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}\,\rho\,dx\,dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\Phi\Bigl[\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\cdot(\rho\nabla\tilde{\phi})-\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho\Bigr]dx\,dt\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\int_{0}^{T}\!\int\Phi\Bigl[\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\cdot(\rho\nabla\tilde{\phi})-\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho\Bigr]dx\,dt\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To connect the PDE dynamics with the training objective, we derive the first-order optimality conditions of (<a href="#S3.E31" title="In 3.2 Stability of the optimization problem ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>), which yield a coupled system</p>
<table id="S3.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}-\partial_{t}\Phi-\nabla\Phi\cdot\nabla\tilde{\phi}-\beta^{-1}\Delta\Phi=0\,,\\
\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\cdot(\rho\nabla\tilde{\phi})-\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho=0\,,\\
\rho(\nabla\tilde{\phi}-\nabla\Phi)=0\,,\\
\Phi_{T}=\tfrac{\rho^{*}}{\rho_{T}}\,,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}-\partial_{t}\Phi-\nabla\Phi\cdot\nabla\tilde{\phi}-\beta^{-1}\Delta\Phi=0\,,\\
\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\cdot(\rho\nabla\tilde{\phi})-\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho=0\,,\\
\rho(\nabla\tilde{\phi}-\nabla\Phi)=0\,,\\
\Phi_{T}=\tfrac{\rho^{*}}{\rho_{T}}\,,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the derivation can be found in the appendix.
The third condition enforces <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\tilde{\phi}=\nabla\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\tilde{\phi}=\nabla\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, which reduces the system to</p>
<table id="S3.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\partial_{t}\tilde{\phi}+\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}+\beta^{-1}\Delta\tilde{\phi}=0\,,\\
\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\cdot(\rho\nabla\tilde{\phi})-\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho=0\,,\\
\tilde{\phi}_{T}=\tfrac{\rho^{*}}{\rho_{T}}\,.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\partial_{t}\tilde{\phi}+\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}+\beta^{-1}\Delta\tilde{\phi}=0\,,\\
\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\cdot(\rho\nabla\tilde{\phi})-\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho=0\,,\\
\tilde{\phi}_{T}=\tfrac{\rho^{*}}{\rho_{T}}\,.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark (Viscosity regularization).</span>
The coupled system (<a href="#S3.E33" title="In 3.2 Stability of the optimization problem ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) consists of a backward HJB equation for <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> with terminal condition <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\phi}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\phi}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a forward Fokker–Planck equation for <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> with initial condition <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Laplacian (score) term in the HJB equation acts as a viscosity term that prevents gradient blow-up.
In the inviscid case <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{-1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the equation</p>
<table id="S3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\tilde{\phi}+\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\tilde{\phi}+\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">generically develops gradient singularities in finite backward time, so the KKT system (<a href="#S3.E32" title="In 3.2 Stability of the optimization problem ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) is well-defined only up to the first shock time.
In contrast, when <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{-1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the viscous HJB equation</p>
<table id="S3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}\tilde{\phi}+\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}+\beta^{-1}\Delta\tilde{\phi}=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}\tilde{\phi}+\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}+\beta^{-1}\Delta\tilde{\phi}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">admits a unique smooth solution <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\phi}\in C^{\infty}([0,T]\times\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\phi}\in C^{\infty}([0,T]\times\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for all finite <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, provided the terminal data <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{T}=\exp(\tfrac{\beta}{2}\tilde{\phi}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{T}=\exp(\tfrac{\beta}{2}\tilde{\phi}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth and strictly positive, ensuring bounded gradients and no blow-up.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example (1D explicit blow-up).</span>
In one dimension, setting <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\partial_{x}\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\partial_{x}\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> reduces the inviscid HJB equation to Burgers’ equation <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}v+v\,\partial_{x}v=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}v+v\,\partial_{x}v=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
For quadratic terminal data <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{T}(x)=-\tfrac{a}{2}x^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{T}(x)=-\tfrac{a}{2}x^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the solution</p>
<table id="S3.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(t,x)=-\frac{a\,x}{1-a\,(T-t)}\quad\text{for}\quad T-\tfrac{1}{a}&lt;t\leq T" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>for</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(t,x)=-\frac{a\,x}{1-a\,(T-t)}\quad\text{for}\quad T-\tfrac{1}{a}&lt;t\leq T</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">blows up as <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\downarrow T-1/a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\downarrow T-1/a</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the inviscid KKT system is well-posed only up to this first shock time, while the viscous case avoids blow-up due to the smoothing effect of the heat operator.
This highlights the stabilizing role of the score function <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-1}\nabla\log\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in the token evolution (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Numerical experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To solve the optimization problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we parameterize the unknown drift potential <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> using a residual neural network. Following the implementation in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, we write</p>
<table id="S4.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(z)=z^{T}N(z,\theta)+\frac{1}{2}z^{T}A^{T}Az+b^{T}z+c\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(z)=z^{T}N(z,\theta)+\frac{1}{2}z^{T}A^{T}Az+b^{T}z+c\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=[x,t]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=[x,t]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(z,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(z,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is a residual network with parameters <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> that captures the nonlinear component of the dynamics, while the remaining terms account for the linear part. This formulation allows both <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\cdot\nabla\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\cdot\nabla\phi</annotation></semantics></math> to be computed explicitly. Combined with the explicit score function update through RWPO, this yields closed-form updates for the iterative steps in (<a href="#S2.E23" title="In 16 ‣ Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E24" title="In 17 ‣ Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the experiments below, we primarily compare samples generated by the standard OT-flow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> with those from our proposed flow model incorporating the sparse transformer in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Additionally, the parameters <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are treated as trainable, allowing the model to select them implicitly during training. Details on model initialization, training parameters, and experimental settings are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Details of numerical experiments ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          The code is in GitHub with the link <a href="https://github.com/fq-han/RWPO-sparse-transformer" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/fq-han/RWPO-sparse-transformer</a>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Benchmark examples</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For this example, we generate new samples for several standard benchmark distributions, including double moon, rings, 2-spirals, 8-Gaussian distributions, and the checkerboard.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
