</span></span></span></p>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of generated samples across benchmark datasets. Each column corresponds to a different dataset.
Top row: target density <math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Middle row: generated samples. Bottom row: KDE-estimated distribution using a Gaussian kernel.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Benchmark examples ‣ 4 Numerical experiments ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the proposed flow model with the sparse transformer accurately and robustly recovers the target benchmark distributions.</p>
</div>
