Columns show the target density and generated samples without and with the sparse transformer, respectively.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Benchmark examples ‣ 4 Numerical experiments ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we observe that the flow with the sparse transformer provides a closer approximation to the benchmark distributions, demonstrating the positive effect of the sparse prior on sample quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Bayesian inverse problems</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now apply the proposed flow model to solve Bayesian inverse problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> denote the parameter of interest and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding measurement, modeled as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=F(x)+\epsilon\,,\qquad\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=F(x)+\epsilon\,,\qquad\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a known forward operator. The objective is to approximate the posterior distribution</p>
<table id="S4.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x\mid y)\;\propto\;p(y\mid x)\,p(x)\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x\mid y)\;\propto\;p(y\mid x)\,p(x)\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with likelihood function <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> and prior <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We employ a conditional flow to approximate the posterior.
Given training pairs <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{T}(x\mid y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{T}(x\mid y)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the learned conditional density.
The training objective is to minimize the KL divergence</p>
<table id="S4.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\big(\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\|\,p(x\mid y)\big)=\int\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\log\frac{\rho_{T}(x\mid y)}{p(x\mid y)}\,dx\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\big(\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\|\,p(x\mid y)\big)=\int\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\log\frac{\rho_{T}(x\mid y)}{p(x\mid y)}\,dx\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Using Bayes’ rule and omitting the constant term <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\big(\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\|\,p(x\mid y)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\big(\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\|\,p(x\mid y)\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\log\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,dx-\int\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\log p(y\mid x)\,dx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\log\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,dx-\int\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\log p(y\mid x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad-\int\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\log p(x)\,dx\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad-\int\rho_{T}(x\mid y)\,\log p(x)\,dx\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Approximating the integrals by Monte Carlo sampling with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{T}\sim\rho_{T}(x\mid y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{T}\sim\rho_{T}(x\mid y)</annotation></semantics></math> yields the empirical loss</p>
<table id="S4.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\;\approx\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Big[\log\rho_{T}(x_{i}^{T}\mid y)-\log p(y\mid x_{i}^{T})-\log p(x_{i}^{T})\Big]\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.170em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\;\approx\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Big[\log\rho_{T}(x_{i}^{T}\mid y)-\log p(y\mid x_{i}^{T})-\log p(x_{i}^{T})\Big]\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{0}\sim\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{0}\sim\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from a simple base distribution, and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained via the evolution (<a href="#S2.E12" title="In 2.2 Regularized Wasserstein proximal operator layer ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) with the drift potential <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{y}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{y}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For this application, the training step in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> corresponds to integrating forward in time from <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, so the system in (<a href="#S2.E23" title="In 16 ‣ Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>)-(<a href="#S2.E24" title="In 17 ‣ Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) is replaced by its forward-time counterpart.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the experiments below, we consider a Gaussian likelihood function and a Laplace prior:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y\mid x)\;\propto\;\exp\!\left(-\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\,\|y-F(x)\|_{2}^{2}\right),\qquad p(x)\;\propto\;\exp(-\lambda\|x\|_{1})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∝</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y\mid x)\;\propto\;\exp\!\left(-\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\,\|y-F(x)\|_{2}^{2}\right),\qquad p(x)\;\propto\;\exp(-\lambda\|x\|_{1})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In this case, the empirical loss simplifies to</p>
<table id="S4.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\;\approx\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Big[\log\rho_{T}(x_{i}\mid y)+\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\|y-F(x_{i})\|_{2}^{2}-\log p(x_{i})\Big]+C\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\;\approx\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Big[\log\rho_{T}(x_{i}\mid y)+\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\|y-F(x_{i})\|_{2}^{2}-\log p(x_{i})\Big]+C\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To accelerate training, we incorporate a supervised term <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x_{i}^{T}-x_{i}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x_{i}^{T}-x_{i}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> into the loss function.
This encourages the flow to map samples more accurately while retaining the theoretical properties established in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Moreover, the transport cost and the HJB regularizer in (<a href="#S2.E16" title="In 2.4 Learning objective and training procedure ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) remain unchanged.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Training the conditional flow with the drift potential <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> thus transports the base distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the conditional law <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{T}(x\mid y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{T}(x\mid y)</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling efficient posterior sampling for new measurements <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the following, we illustrate this approach on three representative inverse problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Elliptic inverse problem.</span>
We first consider the problem of recovering a function <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> from observations <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> governed by the elliptic PDE</p>
<table id="S4.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\alpha\Delta w+w=u\quad\text{in }\Omega\,,\qquad\frac{\partial w}{\partial\nu}=0\quad\text{on }\partial\Omega\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>on </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\alpha\Delta w+w=u\quad\text{in }\Omega\,,\qquad\frac{\partial w}{\partial\nu}=0\quad\text{on }\partial\Omega\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed parameter. The measured data are</p>
<table id="S4.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=w|_{\Gamma}+\zeta=(Fu)|_{\Gamma}+\zeta\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=w|_{\Gamma}+\zeta=(Fu)|_{\Gamma}+\zeta\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\subset\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\subset\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the observation points.
The inverse problem consists of reconstructing <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> from these noisy, partial observations of <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To simplify the recovery, we assume that <math id="S4.I1.i1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Gaussian bump basis expansion</p>
<table id="S4.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x)=\sum_{j=1}^{d}\exp\!\Big(-\frac{|x-x_{j}|^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\Big)\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x)=\sum_{j=1}^{d}\exp\!\Big(-\frac{|x-x_{j}|^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\Big)\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.I1.i1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly distributed in <math id="S4.I1.i1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then apply the proposed sparse transformer to learn the posterior distribution of <math id="S4.I1.i1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/validation_loss_combined.png" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/399;" width="476" height="399" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Validation loss trajectory.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/bump_recovery_iter20000_ifl1=0_lambdak=0.png" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/270;" width="476" height="270" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Recovery without sparse transformer.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/bump_recovery_iter20000_ifl1=1_lambdak=2.png" id="S4.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/270;" width="476" height="270" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Recovery with sparse transformer.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of recovery results. The first plot shows validation loss trajectories, where blue and orange correspond to with and without the sparse transformer. The last two plots show reconstructions without and with the sparse transformer. Dotted: true coefficients; red: sample mean; orange: individual samples. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ item 1 ‣ 4.2 Bayesian inverse problems ‣ 4 Numerical experiments ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the first plot implies the validation loss decreases more rapidly when using the sparse transformer than without it. Moreover, we see that the flow with the sparse transformer provides a more accurate approximation for the solution of the inverse problem. The distribution of the recovered samples in the last plot is also closer to Gaussian, consistent with the underlying noise distribution, as reflected in the spread of the orange curve.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lorenz-63 system.</span>
We examine our flow model on parameter inference for the Lorenz-63 chaotic dynamical system following the setup in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\frac{d}{dt}x_{1}=\sigma(x_{2}-x_{1})\,,\\
\frac{d}{dt}x_{2}=x_{1}(r-x_{3})-x_{2}\,,\\
\frac{d}{dt}x_{3}=x_{1}x_{2}-\beta x_{3}\,,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\frac{d}{dt}x_{1}=\sigma(x_{2}-x_{1})\,,\\
\frac{d}{dt}x_{2}=x_{1}(r-x_{3})-x_{2}\,,\\
\frac{d}{dt}x_{3}=x_{1}x_{2}-\beta x_{3}\,,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with three unknown parameters <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma,r,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma,r,\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. After a spin-up time <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{spin}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>spin</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{spin}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we collect averaged observations over an interval <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{ob}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>ob</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{ob}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i,j}=\int_{T+jT_{\text{ob}}}^{T+(j+1)T_{\text{ob}}}x_{i}(t)\,dt+\zeta_{i}\,,\qquad\zeta_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\text{noise}}^{2})\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>ob</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>ob</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>noise</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i,j}=\int_{T+jT_{\text{ob}}}^{T+(j+1)T_{\text{ob}}}x_{i}(t)\,dt+\zeta_{i}\,,\qquad\zeta_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\text{noise}}^{2})\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0,\dots,n_{mea}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0,\dots,n_{mea}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The inverse problem is to recover <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma,r,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma,r,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> from noisy averaged trajectory data <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/x_est_running_min_combined_reload.png" id="S4.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/408;" width="476" height="408" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Validation loss trajectory.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/parameter_estimates_exp2_reload.png" id="S4.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/408;" width="476" height="408" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Without sparse transformer.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/parameter_estimates_exp1_reload.png" id="S4.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/408;" width="476" height="408" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F5.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">With sparse transformer.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Lorenz-63 parameter inference. The first plot shows the validation error, where blue and orange correspond to with and without the sparse transformer, respectively. The second plot shows parameter estimates (<math id="S4.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>: blue, <math id="S4.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>: orange, <math id="S4.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>: green) without the sparse transformer, and the third plot shows estimates with the sparse transformer; dashed lines indicate true values. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ item 2 ‣ 4.2 Bayesian inverse problems ‣ 4 Numerical experiments ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we observe that the sparse transformer achieves more accurate parameter estimations while converging faster during the training process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Electrical impedance tomography.</span>
Next, we consider the classical electrical impedance tomography (EIT) inverse problem governed by</p>
<table id="S4.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\nabla\cdot(\sigma\nabla w)=0\quad\text{in }\Omega\,,\qquad\begin{cases}w=f\\
\frac{\partial w}{\partial\nu}=g\end{cases}\quad\text{on }\partial\Omega\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>in </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mtext>on </mtext><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\nabla\cdot(\sigma\nabla w)=0\quad\text{in }\Omega\,,\qquad\begin{cases}w=f\\
\frac{\partial w}{\partial\nu}=g\end{cases}\quad\text{on }\partial\Omega\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the conductivity distribution to be recovered.
For simplicity, we parameterize <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> using a Gaussian bump expansion</p>
<table id="S4.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}c_{ij}\,\exp\!\left(-\tfrac{\|x-x_{ij}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}c_{ij}\,\exp\!\left(-\tfrac{\|x-x_{ij}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with fixed grid points <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{ij}\in[0,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{ij}\in[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and unknown coefficients <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c_{ij}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c_{ij}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to be inferred.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We discretize and solve the forward problem using finite differences.
For the inverse problem, we impose multiple boundary voltage patterns <math id="S4.I1.i3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and record the corresponding Neumann data <math id="S4.I1.i3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby generating datasets for conductivity recovery.
Finally, we employ the proposed flow with the sparse transformer to approximate the posterior distribution <math id="S4.I1.i3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\sigma|g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\sigma|g)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda0/eit_recovery_iter1_true.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda0/eit_recovery_iter1_mean64.png" id="S4.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda0/eit_recovery_iter1_error.png" id="S4.F6.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda2/eit_recovery_iter1_mean64.png" id="S4.F6.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda2/eit_recovery_iter1_error.png" id="S4.F6.g5" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda0/eit_recovery_iter2_true.png" id="S4.F6.g6" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda0/eit_recovery_iter2_mean64.png" id="S4.F6.g7" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda0/eit_recovery_iter2_error.png" id="S4.F6.g8" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda2/eit_recovery_iter2_mean64.png" id="S4.F6.g9" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda2/eit_recovery_iter2_error.png" id="S4.F6.g10" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig7" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda0/eit_recovery_iter3_true.png" id="S4.F6.g11" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering">True inclusion</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig8" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda0/eit_recovery_iter3_mean64.png" id="S4.F6.g12" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda0/eit_recovery_iter3_error.png" id="S4.F6.g13" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering">Recovery and Error without sparse transformer</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig9" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda2/eit_recovery_iter3_mean64.png" id="S4.F6.g14" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/lambda2/eit_recovery_iter3_error.png" id="S4.F6.g15" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering">Recovery and Error with sparse transformer</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">EIT reconstructions for four different inclusions (rows). Each row shows the true conductivity (left), reconstructions and error maps without (middle two columns) and with sparse transformer (right two columns).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the three examples in Fig <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ item 3 ‣ 4.2 Bayesian inverse problems ‣ 4 Numerical experiments ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the relative <math id="S4.I1.i3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> errors are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Without sparse transformer: <math id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.99\!\times\!10^{-2},\ 2.15\!\times\!10^{-2},\ 5.42\!\times\!10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.99\!\times\!10^{-2},\ 2.15\!\times\!10^{-2},\ 5.42\!\times\!10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With sparse transformer: <math id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.70\!\times\!10^{-2},\ 1.87\!\times\!10^{-2},\ 4.86\!\times\!10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.70\!\times\!10^{-2},\ 1.87\!\times\!10^{-2},\ 4.86\!\times\!10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">These results show that the network with the sparse transformer consistently achieves more accurate reconstructions across various inclusions, as measured by the relative <math id="S4.I1.i3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> error.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Image generation for MNIST dataset.</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next evaluate the proposed model on the MNIST dataset. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, we adopt an encoder–decoder architecture: the encoder <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B:\mathbb{R}^{784}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>784</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B:\mathbb{R}^{784}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> maps images to a latent representation, and the decoder <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{784}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>784</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{784}</annotation></semantics></math> reconstructs images from this latent space. The encoder–decoder pair is trained independently to satisfy <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(B(x))\approx x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(B(x))\approx x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our flow model is trained to transport the latent prior <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the target distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. To generate new digit images, we sample from <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, transport these samples to <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> using the learned flow, and then decode them with <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/interpMNISTGen_36_L10.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/96;" width="96" height="96" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/interpMNISTGen_18.png" id="S4.F7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/96;" width="96" height="96" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/interpMNISTGen_47.png" id="S4.F7.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/96;" width="96" height="96" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="2510.16356v1/New_figs/interpMNISTGen_36_L11.png" id="S4.F7.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/96;" width="96" height="96" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Digit interpolations on MNIST using the learned flow. The first example is generated without the sparse transformer, and the following three are generated with it.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.3 Image generation for MNIST dataset. ‣ 4 Numerical experiments ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> demonstrates that the sparse transformer effectively learns the MNIST distribution. Compared with the flow trained without the sparse transformer (first plot), which shows limited generalization, our model attains faster convergence and improved generalization quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Summary and discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduced a sparse transformer architecture derived from the regularized Wasserstein proximal operator with an <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> prior distribution.
This formulation embeds problem-specific structural information, such as sparsity, directly into the network through the proximal mapping of the <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> function, thereby improving both the accuracy and interpretability of the learned flow.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theoretical analysis shows that the <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> prior not only enforces sparsity in the learned representations but also accelerates convergence when the target distribution is sparse.
As established in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, this mechanism yields faster KL decay and avoids gradient blow-up.
Experiments in generative modeling and Bayesian inverse problems further demonstrate that the proposed sparse transformer achieves more accurate recovery of target distributions and converges faster than standard neural ODE–based flows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond these results, our framework highlights how regularized Wasserstein proximal sampling methods serve as a unifying tool that bridges ideas from mean field games <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, Wasserstein gradient flows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, and optimal control formulations of transformer architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, thereby extending their reach within machine learning.
In future work, we plan to rigorously analyze the acceleration properties of RWPO-induced flows for more general target distributions under sparse or low-dimensional structures, and to extend this approach to other important priors, such as total variation or structured low-rank regularizations, within broader classes of generative AI and Bayesian inverse problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Postponed derivation and proof</h2>

<div id="A1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 2.3 Sparse transformer with 𝐿_1 prior ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Introduce a Lagrange multiplier <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(t,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(t,x)</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the augmented Lagrangian</span></p>
<table id="A1.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\int_{0}^{h}\int\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\|v\|^{2}\rho+\Phi\left(\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\cdot(\rho v)-\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho\right)\right)\,dx\,dt+\int\psi(x)q(x)\,dx\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\int_{0}^{h}\int\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\|v\|^{2}\rho+\Phi\left(\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\cdot(\rho v)-\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho\right)\right)\,dx\,dt+\int\psi(x)q(x)\,dx\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">The Euler-Lagrange equations yield</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\cdot(\rho\nabla\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\cdot(\rho\nabla\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial_{t}\Phi+\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\Phi\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial_{t}\Phi+\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\Phi\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\beta^{-1}\Delta\Phi\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\beta^{-1}\Delta\Phi\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">with boundary conditions <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(0,x)=\rho_{k}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(0,x)=\rho_{k}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(h,x)=-\psi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(h,x)=-\psi(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying the Hopf-Cole transform</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\exp\!\left(\tfrac{\beta}{2}\Phi\right),\qquad\hat{\eta}=\frac{\rho}{\eta}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mi>η</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\exp\!\left(\tfrac{\beta}{2}\Phi\right),\qquad\hat{\eta}=\frac{\rho}{\eta}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text">decouples the system into forward and backward heat equations with initial and terminal conditions <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(h,x)=\exp(-\tfrac{\beta}{2}\psi(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(h,x)=\exp(-\tfrac{\beta}{2}\psi(x))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(0,x)\hat{\eta}(0,x)=\rho_{k}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(0,x)\hat{\eta}(0,x)=\rho_{k}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Solving via convolution with the heat kernel leads directly to (<a href="#S2.E13" title="In Lemma 1. ‣ 2.3 Sparse transformer with 𝐿_1 prior ‣ 2 Proposed network structure and PDE formulation ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 4. ‣ 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I(t)=I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(t)=I(\rho_{t}\|\rho^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. From (<a href="#S3.E26" title="In 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E27" title="In 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>),</span></p>
<table id="A1.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}(t)=-\beta^{-1}I(t)-\lambda\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})\;\leq\;-\beta^{-1}I(t)-\lambda\gamma\sqrt{I(t)}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}(t)=-\beta^{-1}I(t)-\lambda\mathcal{D}(\rho_{t})\;\leq\;-\beta^{-1}I(t)-\lambda\gamma\sqrt{I(t)}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">By the log-Sobolev inequality, <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(t)\geq a\,y(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(t)\geq a\,y(t)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=2/C_{\mathrm{LS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>LS</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=2/C_{\mathrm{LS}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence</span></p>
<table id="A1.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}(t)\leq-ay(t)-\lambda\gamma\sqrt{a}\,\sqrt{y(t)}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}(t)\leq-ay(t)-\lambda\gamma\sqrt{a}\,\sqrt{y(t)}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the scalar comparison ODE <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}(t)=-au(t)-\lambda\gamma\sqrt{a}\,\sqrt{u(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}(t)=-au(t)-\lambda\gamma\sqrt{a}\,\sqrt{u(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(0)=y(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(0)=y(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Setting <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(t)=\sqrt{u(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(t)=\sqrt{u(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> yields
<math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}(t)=-(a/2)z(t)-(\lambda\gamma/2)\sqrt{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}(t)=-(a/2)z(t)-(\lambda\gamma/2)\sqrt{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have the explicit formula</span></p>
<table id="A1.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(t)=\Big(\sqrt{y(0)}+\tfrac{\lambda\gamma}{\sqrt{a}}\Big)e^{-\tfrac{a}{2}t}-\tfrac{\lambda\gamma}{\sqrt{a}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(t)=\Big(\sqrt{y(0)}+\tfrac{\lambda\gamma}{\sqrt{a}}\Big)e^{-\tfrac{a}{2}t}-\tfrac{\lambda\gamma}{\sqrt{a}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(t)\leq(z(t)_{+})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(t)\leq(z(t)_{+})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, giving (<a href="#S3.E28" title="In Proposition 4. ‣ 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 5. ‣ 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}^{\lambda}(t):=\int\|x\|_{2}^{2}\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}^{\lambda}(t):=\int\|x\|_{2}^{2}\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math>.
Multiplying (<a href="#S3.E25" title="In 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) by <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and integrating by parts gives</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}M_{2}^{\lambda}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}M_{2}^{\lambda}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int\|x\|_{2}^{2}\partial_{t}\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int\|x\|_{2}^{2}\partial_{t}\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-2\int x\cdot\nabla\phi(x)\,\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx-2\int x\cdot\nabla\psi^{\lambda}(x)\,\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx+2d\beta^{-1}\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-2\int x\cdot\nabla\phi(x)\,\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx-2\int x\cdot\nabla\psi^{\lambda}(x)\,\rho_{t}^{\lambda}(x)\,dx+2d\beta^{-1}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\lambda}(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\lambda}(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\!\cdot\!\nabla\psi^{\lambda}(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\!\cdot\!\nabla\psi^{\lambda}(x)=\lambda\|x\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we obtain (<a href="#S3.E29" title="In item (i) ‣ Proposition 5. ‣ 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>), proving (i).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (ii), consider <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\phi(x)=-Ax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\phi(x)=-Ax</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\succeq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⪰</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\succeq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then (<a href="#S3.E29" title="In item (i) ‣ Proposition 5. ‣ 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) becomes</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}M_{2}^{\lambda}(t)=-2\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[X_{t}^{\lambda\top}AX_{t}^{\lambda}\right]-2\lambda\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|X_{t}^{\lambda}\|_{1}\right]+2d\beta^{-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}M_{2}^{\lambda}(t)=-2\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[X_{t}^{\lambda\top}AX_{t}^{\lambda}\right]-2\lambda\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|X_{t}^{\lambda}\|_{1}\right]+2d\beta^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Under a synchronous coupling (shared noise and identical initial data),
the drift <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\lambda\,\mathrm{sign}(X_{t}^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>sign</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\lambda\,\mathrm{sign}(X_{t}^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> exerts a stronger pull toward the origin for larger <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>,
implying that <math id="A1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X_{t}^{\lambda_{1}}|\leq|X_{t}^{\lambda_{2}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X_{t}^{\lambda_{1}}|\leq|X_{t}^{\lambda_{2}}|</annotation></semantics></math> holds coordinatewise almost surely.
Taking expectations yields <math id="A1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}^{\lambda_{1}}(t)\leq M_{2}^{\lambda_{2}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}^{\lambda_{1}}(t)\leq M_{2}^{\lambda_{2}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
which proves (<a href="#S3.E30" title="In item (ii) ‣ Proposition 5. ‣ 3.1 Convergence with the sparse prior ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Derivation of (<a href="#S3.E32" title="In 3.2 Stability of the optimization problem ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>).</span>
Let <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the objective functional in (<a href="#S3.E31" title="In 3.2 Stability of the optimization problem ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>).
The corresponding KKT conditions are obtained by setting the first variations with respect to <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> to zero:</p>
<table id="A1.E41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\dfrac{\delta\mathcal{I}}{\delta\rho}=\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}-\partial_{t}\Phi-\nabla\Phi\!\cdot\!\nabla\tilde{\phi}-\beta^{-1}\Delta\Phi=0\,,\\
\dfrac{\delta\mathcal{I}}{\delta\Phi}=\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\!\cdot\!(\rho\nabla\tilde{\phi})-\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho=0\,,\\
\dfrac{\delta\mathcal{I}}{\delta(\nabla\tilde{\phi})}=\rho(\nabla\tilde{\phi}-\nabla\Phi)=0\,,\\
\ \dfrac{\delta\mathcal{I}}{\delta\rho_{T}}=-\Phi_{T}-\tfrac{\rho^{*}}{\rho_{T}}=0\,,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mpadded style="width:0.663em;" width="0.663em"><mo>∇</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\dfrac{\delta\mathcal{I}}{\delta\rho}=\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\tilde{\phi}\|_{2}^{2}-\partial_{t}\Phi-\nabla\Phi\!\cdot\!\nabla\tilde{\phi}-\beta^{-1}\Delta\Phi=0\,,\\
\dfrac{\delta\mathcal{I}}{\delta\Phi}=\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\!\cdot\!(\rho\nabla\tilde{\phi})-\beta^{-1}\Delta\rho=0\,,\\
\dfrac{\delta\mathcal{I}}{\delta(\nabla\tilde{\phi})}=\rho(\nabla\tilde{\phi}-\nabla\Phi)=0\,,\\
\ \dfrac{\delta\mathcal{I}}{\delta\rho_{T}}=-\Phi_{T}-\tfrac{\rho^{*}}{\rho_{T}}=0\,,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">which leads to (<a href="#S3.E32" title="In 3.2 Stability of the optimization problem ‣ 3 Convergence and stability of flow with sparse transformer ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Details of numerical experiments</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the parameters used for model initialization and training in Table <a href="#A2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Appendix B Details of numerical experiments ‣ Sparse Transformer Architectures via Regularized Wasserstein Proximal Operator with 𝐿_1 Prior" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="A2.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Model and training parameters for different examples.</span></figcaption>
<table id="A2.T1.7" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A2.T1.7.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.7.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T1.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Example</span></th>
<th id="A2.T1.7.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A2.T1.7.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A2.T1.7.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A2.T1.7.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T1.7.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">width</span></th>
<th id="A2.T1.7.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{iters}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">iters</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{iters}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A2.T1.7.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T1.7.1.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">batch size</span></th>
